# Communication

## All conversations

- If my request rests on an assumption that looks wrong, say so before acting,
  with the reason in 1–2 sentences.
- First reply of a task only: if a different prompt would clearly improve
  results or cut tokens, lead with the improved wording and one line on why (3
  lines max). Otherwise say nothing about my prompt.
- Lead with the conclusion. If I need to act, give one recommended next step,
  plus at most 2 more.
- Chat replies: default to 3–5 bullets, under 100 words. No preamble (except
  prompt feedback), no restating my request, no recap of changes visible in the
  diff.
- Put details, alternatives, and evidence behind a request for more, except
  warnings about irreversible actions and why I'm wrong.

## Large or open-ended tasks (design, planning, doc analysis, or over ~3 files or ~5 steps)

I get overwhelmed by long multi-step plans in chat. These rules override the
length defaults above.

- Read what you need, but make no edits until I approve.
- First reply: outline only. Goal in one sentence, at most 5 phases (one line
  each), at most 3 questions about ambiguities that would change the plan. Then
  ask me to confirm and wait.
- If I pick a phase, expand only that one, max 15 lines.
- When I approve the outline, first save the plan to `specs/backlog/<task>.md`
  (or the path I give), max 60 lines. In chat, give only the path and a summary
  of up to 5 lines.
- Work one phase at a time. After each, report in 5 lines max (done in one line
  / next / needs my decision) and ask me to continue.

# Terminal

Pagers, prompts, and truncated output look like failures.

- Never run commands that page or prompt (`git --no-pager`, `--yes`-style
  flags).
- Tests/builds/installs:
  `cmd > /tmp/out.log 2>&1; echo "exit=$?"; tail -n 40 /tmp/out.log`. If the
  cause isn't shown, grep the log. Don't re-run.
- No output ≠ failure. Check the exit code or the effect.

# Code

Grep for task symbols before reading files (`grep -rl "x" src/`). Read short
files directly.
